Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["\<\
Kay has asked me to check the result using a chemical potential to integrate \
over time. 
In this notebook I do some computations related to that.\
\>", "Chapter",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9657218254520054`*^9, 3.9657218493712807`*^9}, {
  3.9657218815918274`*^9, 
  3.9657218954112873`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c1744ade-1916-c94e-82ce-\
0940cbfdd22d"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Borel Resummation", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.965721902873825*^9, 
  3.9657219067911396`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"911ebc22-4ab8-0c48-9e04-\
cc35ec4b20af"],

Cell["\<\
What Kay means by Borel Resummations seems to be the following: 
1) Take your perturbation series in \[Epsilon] and apply Borel to it to \
improve its convergence (essentially you want to be able to use finite \
\[Epsilon])
2) Approximate the result obtained with Pade, i.e. with a function, i.e. \
replace the finite sum with a series. (there exists the function \
PadeApproximant[] in \t\t\t\tmathematica)
3) Do the anti-Borel (i.e. Laplace transform) to come back to something \
similar to the series you stared with, but with improved radius of \
convergence. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9657219183124275`*^9, 
  3.9657221653766937`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"875a5c3d-e67c-3041-8392-\
64b6aa7df8d4"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"$Assumptions", "=."}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9657229247494392`*^9, 3.9657229315463047`*^9}, {
  3.9657259735777817`*^9, 3.9657259768602676`*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[180]:=",ExpressionUUID->"f426eb56-83de-c145-b528-5a98575daba9"],

